The government asked former Health Secretary **Alan Milburn** to lead an **independent review** into why so many young people are **not in education, employment or training** (often called **NEET**). It has a particular focus on **young people with health conditions and disabilities**.

The **interim report**, called **Young people and work**, sets out what the review has found so far. It says a lot about **PIP** and **Universal Credit health**, and some of it is worrying for young disabled people and their families. This guide explains what it found, what it does **not** change, and what you can do.

{% callout type="info" title="The short version" %}
- The review covers people aged **16 to 24** across the UK
- The interim report is a **diagnosis only**. It **makes no recommendations**
- **Nothing has changed** to PIP, Universal Credit or any other benefit because of it
- It says the benefits system **protects those who cannot work**, and that this is **"non-negotiable"**
- But it also says the system does too little to **help young disabled people who want to work**
- A **final report**, with proposals, is due later in 2026
{% /callout %}

## Who the review is about

At the end of 2025, **957,000** young people aged 16 to 24 in the UK were not in education, employment or training. That is about **1 in 8**.

The report says health has become the biggest change:

- **45%** of young people who are NEET say they have a **disability**, up from 21% about ten years earlier
- **44%** report a **work-limiting health condition**, up from 26% in 2015
- among disabled young people who are NEET, **mental health** is now the main condition for **42.6%**, nearly double the figure in 2011
- more young people now list **autism** or a **learning difficulty** as their main condition

Importantly, it also found that most want to work or learn. In a survey for the review, **84%** of young people who are NEET said they want to find a **job, education or training**. The report rejects the idea that young people are a "soft generation" or that mental health is "an excuse".

## What it says about PIP

**PIP** helps with the **extra costs** of a long-term condition or disability. It is not an out-of-work benefit, and the report accepts this. It says PIP "is not an assessment of their ability to work" and that payment "is unrelated to employment status".

Even so, the report is critical of how PIP works for young people. It says:

- about **430,000** people aged 16 to 24 in England and Wales get PIP
- around **£3.2 billion** a year is spent on PIP for this age group, and this is expected to reach **£6.5 billion** by 2031 to 2032, covering about **700,000** young people
- claims are lasting longer: **87%** of young people who started claiming in 2019 were still on PIP five years later, compared with **74%** of the 2013 group
- only **25%** of young people getting PIP at age 24 are in work
- "At no point in a young person's application or journey on PIP are they asked about, or supported to, work"

The report says it is **essential** that the benefits system supports young disabled people to **live independently**. Its argument is that PIP could also come with **practical support**, such as occupational therapy, coaching or help at work, and that many claimants would welcome this. It found **69%** of disabled claimants were open to being contacted by the DWP about offers of support.

Read our guide to [PIP](/benefits/pip/) and [the Timms Review of PIP](/articles/the-timms-review-of-pip-explained/), which is a separate review of PIP.

## What it says about Universal Credit health

The report looks closely at the **Universal Credit health** journey and the **Work Capability Assessment (WCA)**. It found:

- about **257,000** people aged 16 to 24 are on the Universal Credit health journey
- most of those assessed are placed in the **LCWRA** group, which has **no work-related requirements**
- only about **1 in 100** young people in the LCWRA group move into work each month
- young people **waiting for a WCA**, about **48,000**, get the equivalent of only **1 to 2 minutes** of work coach time a week
- around **7 in 10** young people claiming a health and disability benefit are still claiming **a decade later**
- reassessments have not returned to pre-pandemic levels, and there is a backlog of nearly **2 million**

It also says the system is too **"fit or unfit"**. Many young people have conditions that **come and go**, but the system does not cope well with that.

The report notes the changes already made. From **April 2026**, most **new** claimants get the lower health element of **£217.26 a month**, and the standard allowance is rising by more than inflation. Read [changes to health and disability benefits](/benefits/universal-credit-health/changes/).

## The fear of trying work

One of the clearest findings is about **fear**. The report says many young people worry that if they try a job and it does not work out, they could lose their benefits and not get them back:

- nearly **half** of health and disability claimants who had not ruled out work said they were worried about this
- **37%** of people getting PIP but not Universal Credit health said the same
- **45%** of people with a learning disability who wanted a paid job said fear about their benefits was the **biggest thing stopping them**

The report mentions the government's **Right to Try** rules, which mean that trying paid or voluntary work will not, in itself, trigger a reassessment. But it says this does not fully remove the risk, so the fear remains.

## Fit notes and the NHS

The report says **93%** of fit notes in England say a person is **"not fit for work"**, and few suggest what would help. In 2025, over **100,000** young people on Universal Credit gave a fit note and joined the health journey. It argues that the NHS should see helping young people back into education or work as part of recovery.

## What it does not say

It is important to be clear about what this report is **not**:

- it is **not** a decision by the government
- it makes **no recommendations**. The report says solutions will come in the final report
- it does **not** say young disabled people should lose their benefits
- it says the welfare system "must always protect those who cannot work and provide proper financial security for people with severe or enduring disabilities. That is non-negotiable."

Any changes would come later. The government would need to respond, and big changes to benefits would usually need new laws, which Parliament would debate first.

{% callout type="good" title="If you get PIP or Universal Credit health now" %}
Nothing has changed because of this report. Keep reporting any changes in your condition as normal. Do not stop claiming, or put off a claim, because of possible future changes. If a decision is wrong, you can still [challenge it](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/).
{% /callout %}

## Support that exists now

The report describes some help that is already available:

- **Access to Work** can pay for support or equipment at work. The report found only **7,400** people aged 16 to 24 got a payment in 2024 to 2025, so many who could use it may not know about it. Read about [Access to Work](/support/work-and-study/access-to-work/)
- **Youth Hubs** and the **Youth Guarantee** offer extra support for young people looking for work
- **Pathways to Work**, **Connect to Work** and **WorkWell** offer work and health support for disabled people and people with health conditions

## What happens next

The interim report says a **final report** will be published later in 2026. It will set out what the review thinks a better system should look like. We will update this page when it is published and when the government responds.

## What you can do now

- **Check you are getting everything you are entitled to.** Try our free [PIP self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/)
- **If you want to try work,** ask your work coach or an adviser how it would affect your benefits before you start
- **Get free, independent advice** from Citizens Advice, Turn2us or a local welfare rights service
- **Have your say.** Disability organisations often run surveys and campaigns when changes are proposed

{% callout type="danger" title="If you are worried or struggling" %}
Uncertainty about benefits can be very stressful. If you are finding it hard to cope, call **Samaritans** free, any time, on **116 123**. If you or someone else is in danger, call **999**.
{% /callout %}
